Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model might exist, potentially influencing multiple areas. To gain insights into these potential effects, we've examined their impact on the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark theory. By linking the new physics interactions in $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions to the known value of $\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u})$, we've determined that the influence of these interactions on $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ is already constrained by the current $\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u})$ measurement. However, we also found that future enhancements in this measurement or LHCb's pending $\mathcal{B}(\Lambda_b 	o 	au  ar{
u})$ determination, which could become relevant if it deviates from the SM prediction, might provide valuable information on the new physics affecting $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions.
